{"canonical_record":{"canonical_payload_sha256":"3d5e82596be530a6a7fa635dc2ecd7b35796c5adb659b6e52813ca2419a215d3","full_review_record_id":"fullrev_f58a52ae6d3ad07ca0a1177b9330ee84a6f784aebc34031447e26873b2e9c30f","overall_assessment":"The paper provides a theoretical analysis of the convergence inefficiencies associated with increasing mini-batch sizes in SGD and the number of learners in ASGD. The primary contribution is the derivation of non-asymptotic convergence bounds that quantify the performance gap between small and large parallelization settings, offering a theoretical justification for empirical observations of slower convergence with larger batches. The work is positioned as an extension of existing convergence analyses (Ghadimi & Lan, Lian et al.) to highlight specific trade-offs in parallel training.","paper_id":"Bk_zTU5eg","paper_identity_sha256":"f829186ea707ede1625ab3a1fea58b82d23fdc17b5b7f9de6ba6536761bd413f","prompt_identity_sha256":"a6f7009bc11755db84e4f38ac37b78e813bfed425e66c2bd90a881ddad46328f","raw_response_sha256":"c2630f908a021cbbb6b51bf0a023292905959a17512c2b82e44407668694c45c","request_identity_sha256":"a5414f661362e8a0a2261d61f5659eec968ac5475f53928f61a25e7f856e9866","reviewer_identity_sha256":"32b29c26fbf1508e066dfb25b1a9a3fe0654c5869a7da3020e1e8e61940befe3","role":"ORIG","schema_version":"agentreview-full-review-corpus-v4","structural_context_sha256":"d7d6f36c6be4207010786e0b685357d2f1b3424834abfe91058f434157c5a86a","subdimension_sections":[{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-001","SRC-002"],"review_text":"The paper does not introduce a new problem, task, or dataset. Instead, it addresses the well-established problem of optimizing non-convex objective functions using Stochastic Gradient Descent (SGD) and its asynchronous variant (ASGD). The research question focuses on analyzing the impact of mini-batch size and the number of learners on convergence rates, which is a standard topic in optimization theory. The contribution is analytical rather than a novel problem formulation.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-PROB"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-011","SRC-014","SRC-018"],"review_text":"The paper does not propose a new algorithm or architectural design. The methodology involves applying standard SGD and ASGD update rules. The theoretical contribution consists of deriving convergence bounds (Lemma 1, Theorem 2, Theorem 4) by extending existing frameworks from Ghadimi & Lan (2013) and Lian et al. (2015). The analysis is a modification of prior work to account for specific mini-batch and learner configurations, rather than the introduction of a novel optimization algorithm.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-MTH"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-003","SRC-005","SRC-015","SRC-021"],"review_text":"The paper provides a theoretical analysis that quantifies the convergence gap between small and large mini-batches (or learner counts) in the non-asymptotic regime. The authors derive specific factors (e.g., 2Mh / (alpha * sqrt(2) + Ml)) by which convergence guarantees can degrade as parallelism increases. This analysis offers a mathematical justification for the practical observation that larger batches often converge slower, providing a deeper understanding of the trade-offs inherent in parallel SGD. The comparison between SGD and ASGD inefficiencies is a notable analytical insight.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-ANL"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-023","SRC-024","SRC-025"],"review_text":"The experimental setup utilizes the standard CIFAR-10 dataset and a standard convolutional neural network architecture. The evaluation protocol involves training with varying mini-batch sizes and numbers of learners to observe convergence behavior. While the experiments provide empirical support for the theoretical claims, the setup itself is derivative of common practices in deep learning and does not introduce a new benchmark, dataset, or evaluation protocol.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-EXP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-004","SRC-017","SRC-020"],"review_text":"The paper analyzes the combination of standard SGD/ASGD with parallelization strategies (increasing mini-batch size or number of learners). The combination is not presented as a novel method but rather as the subject of analysis. The authors do not propose a new synergistic architecture or a creative combination of techniques to improve performance; instead, they characterize the limitations of existing parallelization approaches.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-COM"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-002","SRC-026"],"review_text":"The paper presents a critical assessment of the efficiency of parallelizing SGD via larger mini-batches or more learners. It demonstrates that there is an inherent inefficiency in convergence behavior when increasing parallelism, even when communication overheads are disregarded. This serves as a critical evaluation of the assumption that parallelism always leads to proportional speedups in convergence, highlighting a limitation of standard parallel SGD approaches.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-NEG"}]},"disposition":"CANONICAL_FULL_REVIEW_COMMITTED","item_commit_sha256":"eb63d5356058f58f641bcd13e63ad1800024cd42cb52eb1dc528ac7d401e3fd6","paper_id":"Bk_zTU5eg","paper_ordinal":56,"raw_attempts":[{"assistant_content_base64":"","assistant_content_sha256":"c2630f908a021cbbb6b51bf0a023292905959a17512c2b82e44407668694c45c","attempt_index":0,"error":"","finish_reason":"stop","paper_id":"Bk_zTU5eg","raw_provider_response_base64":"","raw_provider_response_sha256":"259c3a82a37389387375e520956f7dfffaf1917e0575a29a00ce864a45ee65f5","request_identity_sha256":"a5414f661362e8a0a2261d61f5659eec968ac5475f53928f61a25e7f856e9866","role":"ORIG","schema_version":"no-memory-full-review-corpus-build-v6","usage":{"completion_tokens":1064,"prompt_tokens":16059,"prompt_tokens_details":null,"total_tokens":17123},"valid":true}],"request_identity_sha256":"a5414f661362e8a0a2261d61f5659eec968ac5475f53928f61a25e7f856e9866","role":"ORIG","role_ordinal":3,"schema_version":"corpus-build-bootstrap-checkpoint-v4"}